    "summary": "High‑energy set with epic He's Gone > Truckin' > Playing jam and tight Loose Lucy",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a scorching performance of Eyes Of The World, Loose Lucy and China Doll, while the second set builds into an extended He's Gone > Truckin' > Playing In The Band sequence. Jerry’s vocals are smooth with occasional slips, Phil’s bass shines throughout, and the band maintains high energy despite the heat.",
    "review": "June 9 1973 at RFK Stadium finds the Dead in top form, firing on all cylinders from the opening of The Promised Land through a fiery second set. Highlights include a soaring Eyes Of The World where Phil’s bass drives an expansive groove, a razor‑sharp Loose Lucy that rides Brent’s (Keith’s) subtle keyboard textures, and a hauntingly beautiful China Doll. The night truly erupts in the 49‑minute He’s Gone > Truckin' > Playing In The Band suite—a marathon of improvisation that showcases Jerry’s adventurous soloing, Bill’s propulsive drumming and the band’s collective telepathy. A memorable moment comes at the end of Truckin', when Jerry teases a Here Comes Sunshine riff before diving back into the jam, cementing this performance as one of the era’s most vivid. Throughout, vocal duties shift smoothly—Jerry’s voice remains smooth despite a few lyric slips, Phil delivers a jaw‑dropping lead on Box of Rain, and Bob provides solid rhythm support. The heat of the stadium never dampens the band’s intensity; instead it fuels an electric atmosphere captured best by the high‑quality soundboard recordings.",
